---
title: Ultimate Braised Pork Belly (Hong Shao Rou 红烧肉)
description: A combined approach to the classic red-braised pork, utilizing the traditional 'chaotanse' caramelizing method and whole-boil cutting technique for perfectly structured, melt-in-your-mouth cubes.
prep time: 20 minutes
cook time: 2 hours
servings: 4
tags:
  - braised pork belly
  - chinese
  - hong shao rou
course: Main
---

> Hong Shao Rou is all about the 'tangse' (caramel) and the low, slow braise. The addition of dried chilis and Sichuan peppercorns adds a subtle Hunan-style fragrance, but won't make the dish spicy! Don't stress if you can't find skin-on pork belly, but it does add a fantastic gelatinous texture to the final dish.

== Preparation ==

Add the whole block of @pork belly{600%g} (skin-on, half lean/half fat) to a #wok{} and add enough @water{} to reach halfway up the pork -- Boiling the pork whole first firms it up, making it much easier to cut into perfect, structurally sound cubes later.

Boil for ~{2%minutes} on each side until the outside turns opaque. 

Remove the pork, let it cool slightly, and cut into large 5 cm (2-inch) cubes. Reserve the hot water in a separate #claypot{} or heavy-bottomed pot [- It is critical to use hot water for the braise so the pork doesn't seize up and become tough! -]. Clean out your wok.

== Aromatics & Caramel ==

In the clean wok over low heat, briefly fry @dried Sichuan chilis{2}(deseeded) and @Sichuan peppercorns{12} until fragrant. Transfer them, along with @ginger{5%slices}, @garlic{2%cloves}(crushed), @star anise{2}, and @cinnamon stick{1/2} into the claypot with the reserved hot water.

In the wok over low heat, make the caramel (chaotanse). Add @oil{1%tbsp} and crushed @rock sugar{3%tbsp} (about 40g). Melt the sugar into the oil until it turns a dark brown color -- Keep a close eye on this, as sugar can burn from zero to midnight in a blink!

== Braising ==

Carefully add the pork chunks into the wok with the caramel. Toss gently to get a nice coating on each piece, being careful not to break the structure of the fat and meat. 

Turn off the heat, and stir in @Shaoxing wine{3%tbsp}, @dark soy sauce{1/4%cup}, and @light soy sauce{2%tbsp}. 

Transfer everything from the wok into your claypot with the hot water and aromatics. The liquid should cover the pork.

Cover and simmer on low heat for ~{90%minutes}. Try not to peek too much, but check occasionally to ensure it's not boiling dry. The pork is done when a chopstick can pierce it with very little resistance.

== Finishing ==

Uncover the pot, add the remaining @rock sugar{2%tbsp} (or brown sugar), and let it dissolve. 

Turn up the heat slightly and reduce the sauce uncovered for ~{15-20%minutes}. We want a thin, sticky sauce, not a thick stew. 

Remove the pork and strain the sauce into a separate bowl. Brush the pork chunks with the strained sauce to give them a brilliant sheen, and drizzle a spoonful over the top before serving -- Don't waste the extra sauce! Soak hard-boiled eggs in it overnight for a fantastic breakfast.

